  "final_text": "Both the dashboard and the alert rule now exist in Kibana.\n\n**Dashboard: \"EFF-7b477149 Web logs overview\"** (5 panels, time range last 1 year)\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique client IPs (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Average bytes per request (average of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time (line, date histogram on `timestamp`)\n- Responses by status code (pie, terms on `response.keyword`, top 10)\n\n**Alert rule: \"EFF-7b477149 5xx spike\"** (`.es-query`, id `ea4d8b07-ced9-4599-86b7-988f62b99a28`)\n- Runs every 1m against `kibana_sample_data_logs` on `timestamp`.\n- Counts documents where `response.keyword` is between `500` and `599`, over a 5-minute window.\n- Fires when the count is greater than 10.\n- Enabled. Its status is currently \"pending\", which is expected until the first run completes.\n\nChoices I made:\n- **Enabled the rule.** The API creates it disabled by default. You asked for a rule that checks every minute and fires, so I turned it on. No connectors exist in this space, so it has no actions and sends no notifications.\n- **Used `response.keyword`.** The data view lists `response` as a text field with a `.keyword` subfield, so I filtered and aggregated on the keyword version.\n- **Set the dashboard to the last year.** I couldn't confirm when the sample data's timestamps fall, so I chose a wide window. Narrow it if the panels look empty.\n\nNot verified: I couldn't run an ES|QL query against the cluster. The Dev Tools proxy is disabled here, and the Kibana ES|QL route returned 404. So I haven't confirmed the response code values or that the 5xx range matches real data. The first run of the rule and the dashboard panels will show whether it does.",
